Kubernetes provides built-in service discovery via Services and DNS. Mechanisms: 1) Service creates stable endpoint (ClusterIP), 2) CoreDNS provides DNS records, 3) Environment variables injected to pods. Example:
apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: payment-service
spec:
  selector:
    app: payment
  ports:
  - port: 8080
    targetPort: 8080

Service discovery methods:
1) DNS (recommended):
# From any pod:
curl http://payment-service:8080/api/payment
# Fully qualified:
curl http://payment-service.default.svc.cluster.local:8080

2) Environment variables:
PAYMENT_SERVICE_SERVICE_HOST=10.96.0.10
PAYMENT_SERVICE_SERVICE_PORT=8080

Java Spring Boot:
@Configuration
public class ServiceConfig {
  @Value("${payment.service.url:http://payment-service:8080}")
  private String paymentServiceUrl;

  @Bean
  public RestTemplate restTemplate() {
    return new RestTemplate();
  }
}

@Service
public class OrderService {
  @Value("${payment.service.url}")
  private String paymentUrl;

  public void processOrder(Order order) {
    restTemplate.postForEntity(paymentUrl + "/api/payment", paymentRequest, PaymentResponse.class);
  }
}

Headless Service for direct pod access (StatefulSets):
apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: postgres
spec:
  clusterIP: None  # Headless
  selector:
    app: postgres
  ports:
  - port: 5432

Resolves to pod IPs: postgres-0.postgres.default.svc, postgres-1.postgres.default.svc. Service discovery enables loose coupling, dynamic scaling.

Operators extend Kubernetes to manage complex applications using custom resources and controllers. Encode operational knowledge (backup, upgrade, failover) in software. Use Operator when: 1) Managing stateful applications (databases, message queues), 2) Complex lifecycle management, 3) Application-specific operations, 4) Domain expertise automation. Example: PostgreSQL Operator:

Custom Resource Definition (CRD):
apiVersion: apiextensions.k8s.io/v1
kind: CustomResourceDefinition
metadata:
  name: postgresqls.database.example.com
spec:
  group: database.example.com
  versions:
  - name: v1
    served: true
    storage: true
    schema:
      openAPIV3Schema:
        type: object
        properties:
          spec:
            type: object
            properties:
              version:
                type: string
              replicas:
                type: integer
              storage:
                type: string

Custom Resource:
apiVersion: database.example.com/v1
kind: PostgreSQL
metadata:
  name: my-database
spec:
  version: "15"
  replicas: 3
  storage: "100Gi"
  backup:
    schedule: "0 2 * * *"  # Daily 2 AM
    retention: 7

Operator controller (Go):
func (r *PostgreSQLReconciler) Reconcile(ctx context.Context, req ctrl.Request) (ctrl.Result, error) {
  var postgres databasev1.PostgreSQL
  if err := r.Get(ctx, req.NamespacedName, &postgres); err != nil {
    return ctrl.Result{}, client.IgnoreNotFound(err)
  }

  // Create StatefulSet
  statefulSet := &appsv1.StatefulSet{
    ObjectMeta: metav1.ObjectMeta{
      Name: postgres.Name,
      Namespace: postgres.Namespace,
    },
    Spec: appsv1.StatefulSetSpec{
      Replicas: &postgres.Spec.Replicas,
      // ... PostgreSQL configuration
    },
  }

  if err := r.Create(ctx, statefulSet); err != nil {
    return ctrl.Result{}, err
  }

  // Setup backup CronJob
  backupCron := &batchv1.CronJob{
    Spec: batchv1.CronJobSpec{
      Schedule: postgres.Spec.Backup.Schedule,
      // ... backup logic
    },
  }

  return ctrl.Result{}, nil
}

Operator creates StatefulSet, Services, backup CronJobs automatically. Handles upgrades, scaling, backups. Existing operators: Prometheus Operator, Kafka Operator, Elastic Operator. Build custom operator for: proprietary applications, specific business logic, complex operational patterns. Frameworks: Operator SDK, Kubebuilder. Don't build for: simple stateless apps (use Helm), already-existing operators.
